Z||000007||Monkeyman||01-05-2001||09:56 PM||||I seriously considered the same thing.  I finally realized that either I'd have to mount the stereo basically over the auto shifter or cut away part of the center hump.  The stereo will be too deep otherwise.||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||01-07-2001||11:22 AM||||Hmmm.  How much is to deep?<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-=-<BR>-=[ Come out swingin' ]=-||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||01-07-2001||01:05 PM||||Not if you use a CD changer.. you can get a head unit with a detachable face, and run wires from each of the face contacts to the unit, and then you just have to mount the faceplate, which is only 1" deep or so. the rest of the head unit can be mounted somewhere else.<P>Loki originally mentioned this idea to me btw.<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000010||Seanh||01-07-2001||04:05 PM||||We did that once. We didnt use a changer however, we use a detachable face/flip face unit and made a custom plate to snap down where the face plate used to be and had a cd opening in it, and to hide the faceplate contacts. Then mount the unit somewhere else and still use its internal CD Player. Dont know where you would mount it though. Just an idea.||||reg||1
Z||000011||DaRkLoRD||01-07-2001||05:00 PM||||Only reason I suggested a changer was so you wouldn't have a head unit face with a CD slot, but nothing behind it.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Philphine||01-07-2001||07:09 PM||||i want to do that detatched faceplate so bad. i have a pioneer 6disc indash changer, but it needs a head unit to control it. there isn't enough space for both (not to mention the 1/2 din eq in with them). i may get brave and risk destroying a good head unit to try it.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||01-07-2001||07:39 PM||||why would it need to be destroyed?<P>I'm sure some adapter boards could be made on a PC board with IC sockets, to plug into the face and unit.. that's what I'd do.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000014||p4n1c||01-08-2001||11:33 AM||||Head units are usually 9-10" deep, correct?  If it's installed a little further up on the angled piece(not in the center) it should fit correctly, a custom bracket with a plug on the rear for just sliding and snapping the head unit into place without fighting to connect wires would work well.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-=-<BR>-=[ Come out swingin' ]=-||||reg||1
